Service 1 reacts when new data is added to the DB (watching).
MongoDB has a built-in feature for watching: change streams (event-based trigger).
Change streams only work when MongoDB is running on a replica set.

1. Use docker to start MongoDB as a replica set (this part is in docker-compose.yaml):
docker run -d --name mongodb \
  -p 27017:27017 \
  mongo:latest \
  mongod --replSet rs0 --bind_ip_all
This starts MongoDB in replica-set mode and allows connections from other containers.

2. Initialize the replica set:
docker exec -it mongodb mongosh
rs.initiate() <-- inside mongosh

3. insert_data.py script is used to create the database and collection.
The script is also used to insert data into the collection.

4. Service 1 connects to the database using a connection string:
client = MongoClient("mongodb://mongodb:27017/?replicaSet=rs0")
* mongodb:// * use MongoDB protocol
* mongodb * host name of the container hosting the database
* 27017 * default MongoDB port
* ?replicaSet=rs0 * connect using replica set mode

5. Service 1 watches the collection for insertions.
Once an insertion occurs, service 1 is triggered to automatically validate
the inserted document.
If all the data is valid, it triggers service 2 or 3, depending on the letter type.




FINAL STEPS:
a) Start all containers: docker compose up --build -d
b) Initialize the replica set:
   - Open a Mongo shell inside the MongoDB container: docker exec -it mongodb mongosh
   - Then run:
     rs.initiate({
      _id: "rs0",
      members: [
        { _id: 0, host: "mongodb:27017" }
      ]
    })
c) Run insert_data.py